Graph representation\par
\b0\fs24 A graph is a data structure that consist a sets of vertices (called nodes) and edges. There are two ways to store Graphs into the computer's memory:\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl240\slmult1\qj Sequential representation (or, Adjacency matrix representation)\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Linked list representation (or, Adjacency list representation)\par

\pard\sa200\sl240\slmult1\qj\b\fs28 Sequential representation\par
\b0\fs24 In sequential representation, there is a use of an adjacency matrix to represent the mapping between vertices and edges of the graph. We can use an adjacency matrix to represent the undirected graph, directed graph, weighted directed graph, and weighted undirected graph.\par
If adj[i][j] = w, it means that there is an edge exists from vertex i to vertex j with weight w.\par
An entry Aij in the adjacency matrix representation of an undirected graph G will be 1 if an edge exists between Vi and Vj. If an Undirected Graph G consists of n vertices, then the adjacency matrix for that graph is n x n, and the matrix A = [aij] can be defined as :\par
aij = 1 \{if there is a path exists from Vi to Vj\}\par
aij = 0 \{Otherwise\}\par
It means that, in an adjacency matrix, 0 represents that there is no association exists between the nodes, whereas 1 represents the existence of a path between two edges.If there is no self-loop present in the graph, it means that the diagonal entries of the adjacency matrix will be 0.\par
