\b\fs32 Graph Traversals:\par
\b0\fs24 traversing a graph means visiting all the vertices in a graph exactly one, in tree traversal , there a special node root, from which the traversal starts. but in graph all node are treated equally. so the starting node in graph chosen arbitrarily.\par
Graph traversal is a process of visiting all the vertices (nodes) of a graph in a systematic manner. It is used to explore graphs in different applications like search engines, social networks, and pathfinding in maps.\par
There are two primary techniques for traversing a graph:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl240\slmult1\qj\b Depth First Search (DFS)\fs32\par
{\pntext\f0 2.\tab}\fs24 Breadth First Search (BFS)\fs32\par

\pard\sa200\sl240\slmult1\qj Depth First Traversal\par
\b0\fs24 DFS traversal of a graph produces a spanning tree as final result. Spanning tree is a graph without any loops. we use stack data structure with maximum size of total number of vertices in the graph to implement DFS traversal of a graph.It starts at an arbitrary node, marking it as visited.Then, it continues recursively by visiting unmarked adjacent nodes.\par
\b\fs28 Algorithm for DFS\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl240\slmult1\qj\b0\fs24 Start\par
{\pntext\f0 2.\tab}Define a Stack of size equal to the total number of vertices in the graph.\par
{\pntext\f0 3.\tab}Select any vertex as the starting point for traversal. Push it onto the stack.\par
{\pntext\f0 4.\tab}Visit any adjacent vertex of the top of the stack that is not visited and push it onto the stack.\par
{\pntext\f0 5.\tab}Repeat step 4 until there are no new vertices to visit from the vertex on top of the stack.\par
{\pntext\f0 6.\tab}When there is no new vertex to be visited, then use backtracking: pop one vertex from the stack.\par
{\pntext\f0 7.\tab}Repeat steps 4, 5, and 6 until the stack becomes empty.\par
{\pntext\f0 8.\tab}When the stack becomes empty, produce the final spanning tree by removing unused edges from the graph.\par
{\pntext\f0 9.\tab}Stop\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\b\fs32 BFS Traversal \par
\b0\fs24 Breadth-first search is a graph traversal algorithm that starts traversing the graph from the root node and explores all the neighboring nodes. Then, it selects the nearest node and explores all the unexplored nodes. While using BFS for traversal, any node in the graph can be considered as the root node.\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\b Approach\b0 : Queue\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}\b Order\b0 : Level by level (Visit all neighbors of a node before moving to their neighbors)\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}\b Use cases\b0 : Shortest path in unweighted graphs, finding connected components.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}\b Time complexity\b0 : O(V + E)\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj Time complexity of BFS depends upon the data structure used to represent the graph. The time complexity of BFS algorithm is O(V+E), since in the worst case, BFS algorithm explores every node and edge. In a graph, the number of vertices is O(V), whereas the number of edges is O(E).The space complexity of BFS can be expressed as O(V), where V is the number of vertices.\par
\par
\b Algorithm:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\b0 Start at the source vertex.\par
{\pntext\f0 2.\tab}Enqueue the vertex and mark it as visited.\par
{\pntext\f0 3.\tab}While the queue is not empty:- Dequeue a vertex and process it. For each unvisited adjacent vertex, mark it and enqueue it.\b\par

